The CSIPHY termination pulse that mainline never writes

WHAT IS MISSING
---------------
In the CSIPHY common sequence, the vendor writes FIVE registers before walking
the lane table:

    CTRLn(5)  @ 0x0814 = lane mask | CLK_ENABLE
    CTRLn(6)  @ 0x0818 = 0x01    COMMON_PWRDN_B
    CTRLn(7)  @ 0x081C = 0x52    (0x02 on the older versions)
    CTRLn(0)  @ 0x0800 = 0x02    PHY core power-up
    CTRLn(33) @ 0x0884 = 0x01    TERMINATION PULSE

Mainline writes the first three and then overwrites CTRLn(0) with 0x00, and
0x0884 IS NEVER WRITTEN. Without it the PHY stays clamped at the upper level:
the sensor transmits, the PHY hears the lanes, and not one start-of-transmission
sync reaches the CSID.

WHERE IT COMES FROM
-------------------
From https://github.com/asidko/qcom-camss-mainline, which found it on an SM7125
(Atoll, CSIPHY v1.2.2), a sibling of this SoC. It describes the fault word for
word:

    "sensor s_stream(1) + CCI writes and regulators complete, but the CSID0 IRQ
     counter stays at zero during a multi-second STREAMON and the capture file
     is 0 bytes"

Which is exactly what was measured here throughout the investigation.

On v1.2.1 (lane_regs_sm8250) these terminators are repeated INSIDE the lane
table; on v1.2 and v1.2.2 they go once in the common block, which is precisely
the piece mainline writes by hand and where they were left out.

Our lane_regs_sm7150 does write 0x0800 = 0x02, five times, once per lane -that
is why 0060 already covered that half-. 0x0884 does not appear even once.

It sits behind a parameter in 0644 so the attribution can be confirmed without
recompiling.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c	2026-08-17 01:10:47.338745677 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c	2026-08-17 01:11:11.798443774 +0200
@@ -1080,6 +1080,11 @@
  * reiniciar, que en este movil cuesta caro: se cuelga uno de cada cuatro
  * arranques.
  */
+static int pulso_terminacion = 1;
+module_param(pulso_terminacion, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(pulso_terminacion,
+	"Escribir 0x01 en CTRL33 (0x0884), el pulso de terminacion que mainline se deja. 1 = si.");
+
 static int releer_registros;
 module_param(releer_registros, int, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(releer_registros,
@@ -1369,6 +1374,37 @@
 			       CSIPHY_3PH_CMN_CSI_COMMON_CTRLn(regs->offset, 0));
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * EL PULSO DE TERMINACION QUE FALTABA.
+	 *
+	 * CTRLn(33), o sea 0x0884, vale 0x01 en la secuencia comun del
+	 * fabricante y mainline NO LO ESCRIBE NUNCA. Sin el, el PHY se queda
+	 * pinzado a nivel superior: el sensor transmite, el PHY oye los
+	 * carriles, y al CSID no le llega ni un sincronismo de inicio.
+	 *
+	 * Es exactamente el fallo que se veia aqui, y lo describe igual quien
+	 * lo encontro en un SM7125 (Atoll, CSIPHY v1.2.2), que es el hermano de
+	 * este SoC:
+	 *
+	 *   "sensor s_stream(1) + escrituras CCI + reguladores completan, pero
+	 *    el contador de IRQ del CSID0 se queda a cero durante un STREAMON de
+	 *    varios segundos y el fichero de captura son 0 bytes"
+	 *
+	 * En la v1.2.1 (lane_regs_sm8250) estos terminadores van repetidos
+	 * dentro de la tabla de carriles; en la v1.2 y la v1.2.2 van una sola
+	 * vez en el bloque comun, que es justo el trozo que mainline escribe a
+	 * mano y donde se quedaron fuera.
+	 *
+	 * Nuestra lane_regs_sm7150 escribe 0x0800 = 0x02 cinco veces (una por
+	 * carril), asi que ese ya estaba; 0x0884 no aparece ni una vez.
+	 *
+	 * Ajustable en caliente para poder confirmar la atribucion sin
+	 * recompilar.
+	 */
+	if (pulso_terminacion)
+		writel_relaxed(0x01, csiphy->base +
+			       CSIPHY_3PH_CMN_CSI_COMMON_CTRLn(regs->offset, 33));
+
 	if (csiphy_is_gen2(csiphy->camss->res->version))
 		csiphy_gen2_config_lanes(csiphy, settle_cnt);
